# DESCRIPTION

The bootfs service serves the boot image, which the loader put in memory beside the kernel, as a tree of files.
Each file of the image is there at its path in the image: the programs in `bin/`, the shared objects in `lib/`, the manifests in `etc/manifests/`, the drivers' match records in `etc/drivers`, the manual in `man/`, and the rest.
A file of no length is an empty file.
Every other file is a read only slice of the image's memory, so a read or a map sees the image's own pages, which every process that maps the file shares, and nothing can write them.
That memory is named after the file's path (its first 31 bytes), so a process's mappings of a program say which it is (proc(4)'s `trace/procs`).
Node gives the file as memory (`memory`), which a process maps; the image's programs and libraries may also be run from it.

A manifest (manifest(6)) mounts the tree's `bin/` at `/bin`, so that a process starts a program by finding it there, and its `lib/` at `/lib`, where a program that needs libraries finds them (linkmap(6)):

```text
mount /bin bootfs/bin
mount /lib bootfs/lib
```

The service is started with the boot image itself, which only a manifest's `bootfs` directive gives; it exits with a message, and the launcher restarts it, if it has none.
Its own manifest is

```text
service bootfs
program bootfs
bootfs
export
early
restart on-failure
```

The service is early because the services that follow run from it.

# FILES

: `bin/<name>`
  A program of the image.
: `lib/<name>`
  A shared object.
: `etc/manifests/<name>.manifest`
  A service's manifest.
: `etc/drivers`
  The drivers' match records, all in one file.
: `status`
  `serving <n> files`, a line, <n> the number of files in the image.

